Q: How to load multiple API responses in one object A: <h2>Answer: Accumulate Data in a &quot;Master&quot; Container</h2>
<p>The issue with your current approach is that <code>result = JsonConvert.DeserializeObject&lt;OrdersResponse&gt;(json);</code> <strong>overwrites</strong> the variable on every loop. To fix this, you need to treat your <code>OrdersResponse</code> as a &quot;Master&quot; container and append the data from each API call to it.</p>
<h3>1. The Data Structure</h3>
<p>Assuming your <code>OrdersResponse</code> object looks something like this in Java, it must contain a <code>List</code> to hold the collection of items:</p>
<p>Java</p>
<pre class="lang-java prettyprint-override"><code>public class OrdersResponse {
    private int total;
    private boolean hasMore;
    private List&lt;Order&gt; orders = new ArrayList&lt;&gt;();

    // Getters and Setters
    public boolean hasMore() { return hasMore; }
    public List&lt;Order&gt; getOrders() { return orders; }
    // ...
}
</code></pre>
<h3>2. The Logic: Loop and Append</h3>
<p>Instead of replacing the <code>result</code> object, you should deserialize each page into a &quot;partial&quot; object, then move its items into your &quot;master&quot; result.</p>
<p>Java</p>
<pre><code>public static OrdersResponse GetAllOrders()
{
    // 1. Initialize the &quot;Master&quot; object that will hold everything
    OrdersResponse masterResult = new OrdersResponse();
    masterResult.Orders = new List&lt;Order&gt;(); // Ensure the internal list is ready

    int limit = 500;
    int offset = 0;
    bool hasMore = true;

    while (hasMore)
    {
        // 2. Build URL with the current offset
        string url = $&quot;https://myurl123456/?limit={limit}&amp;offset={offset}&quot;;
        HttpWebRequest httpWebRequest = CreateAPIRequest(url);

        using (HttpWebResponse response = (HttpWebResponse)httpWebRequest.GetResponse())
        {
            using (StreamReader reader = new StreamReader(response.GetResponseStream()))
            {
                string json = reader.ReadToEnd();

                // 3. Deserialize this specific page into a temporary &quot;Partial&quot; object
                var partialResponse = JsonConvert.DeserializeObject&lt;OrdersResponse&gt;(json);

                if (partialResponse != null &amp;&amp; partialResponse.Orders != null)
                {
                    // 4. CRITICAL: Append the new items to the Master list instead of overwriting
                    masterResult.Orders.AddRange(partialResponse.Orders);

                    // 5. Update loop conditions from the API response
                    hasMore = partialResponse.hasMore; 
                    offset += limit;
                }
                else
                {
                    hasMore = false;
                }
            }
        }
    }

    return masterResult;
}
</code></pre>
<h3>Key Changes Made:</h3>
<ol>
<li><p><strong>Avoids Overwriting:</strong> By using <code>masterResult.Orders.AddRange()</code>, you are growing the list inside the object rather than replacing the <code>masterResult</code> reference itself.</p>
</li>
<li><p><strong>Pagination Logic:</strong> The <code>while(hasMore)</code> loop ensures that as long as the API indicates there is more data, the code calculates the next <code>offset</code> and makes another call.</p>
</li>
<li><p><strong>Single Object Return:</strong> When the loop finishes, <code>masterResult</code> contains the combined data from every API call made.</p>
</li>
</ol>

Q: How to pass two objects to a view A: <p>Yes, passing multiple objects as parameter is poosible but I am afraid the syntax you mentioned above is not correct.</p>

<p>Try:</p>

<pre><code>$data['object1'] = $object1; 
$data['object2'] = $object2;
$this-&gt;load-&gt;view('myView', $data);
</code></pre>

<p>And on the view page access it as <code>$object1</code> and <code>$object2</code></p>

<p>Adding <strong>Dynamic Data</strong> to the View
Data is passed from the controller to the view by way of an array or an object in the second parameter of the view loading function. Here is an example using an array:</p>

<pre><code>$data = array(
               'title' =&gt; 'My Title',
               'heading' =&gt; 'My Heading',
               'message' =&gt; 'My Message'
          );

$this-&gt;load-&gt;view('blogview', $data);
</code></pre>

<p>And here's an example using an object:</p>

<pre><code>$data = new Someclass();
$this-&gt;load-&gt;view('blogview', $data);
</code></pre>

